            <ArticleTitle>Suppurative iliac lymphadenitis masquerading as appendicitis in an HIV-infected patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>431-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1258/ijsa.2008.008415</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An HIV-infected man presented with acute onset of right iliac fossa pain. Initial assessment of clinical and computed tomography findings suggested a diagnosis of appendicitis. A macroscopically normal appendix was removed. Further imaging performed postoperatively because of persistent symptoms showed a right iliac fossa mass; culture of pus aspirated from the mass grew group A beta-haemolytic Streptococcus confirming a diagnosis of suppurative iliac lymphadenitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
